WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.162
Welcome to Module 8, Strings and Dates in PostgreSQL.

00:00:04.162 --> 00:00:10.977
Text and time values often look simple on the screen, but they carry important analytical meaning.

00:00:10.977 --> 00:00:14.622
In this module, we will clean text carefully, parse structured pieces from strings,

00:00:14.622 --> 00:00:23.532
use regular expressions when simple functions are not enough, choose appropriate date and time types,

00:00:23.532 --> 00:00:26.982
calculate intervals, group events by time grain, and work with time zones.

00:00:26.982 --> 00:00:32.637
Every SQL example can run directly in the browser, and you can copy the same query into pgAdmin

00:00:32.637 --> 00:00:37.127
for additional practice.

00:00:37.127 --> 00:00:42.902
Keep a real project in mind as we work through this section—for example, a CRM standardizing names, emails,

00:00:42.902 --> 00:00:44.532
and phone numbers.

00:00:44.532 --> 00:00:51.327
The technical feature matters because it changes what questions we can answer reliably and how easily another

00:00:51.327 --> 00:00:54.822
analyst can understand our work.

00:00:54.822 --> 00:00:59.122
As you listen, try to connect each SQL or data concept to a decision you might need to

00:00:59.122 --> 00:01:04.802
defend later: what the data means, what one row represents, what can be missing,

00:01:04.802 --> 00:01:08.800
and what assumptions the transformation introduces.
